orm_tools` repeatedly or per individual step.\n3. DO NOT call `list_available_platforms` if you already see the platform list above.\n4. If the raw SOP only involves native OS/system CLI commands (e.g. `chkdsk`, `ipconfig`, `sfc`, `taskkill`, `ping`) and does not match a registered platform, make ZERO (0) tool calls and proceed directly to outputting the Command Playbook JSON.\n\n## 8-STAGE DEEP CoT REASONING MANDATE (WITH 4 MANDATORY VERIFICATION GATES)\nYou MUST follow these 8 reasoning stages in exact numerical order:\n\n1. **Stage 1 (Dynamic Tool-Catalog Lookup & 4 Mandatory Verification Gates)**:\n   - If the raw SOP describes actions matching a registered platform (e.g. 'solandra', 'auth0', 'email', 'airretailer', 'mock'), invoke `get_platform_tools(platform_name=...)` ONCE to retrieve all active tool functions and parameter schemas.\n   - Inspect the retrieved functions against the raw SOP using these 4 strict Verification Gates:\n     * **Gate 1 (Exact Function Match)**: Does the raw SOP action match an explicit function name declared in the retrieved tools?\n     * **Gate 2 (Active Provider Match)**: Is the provider name active and registered in the catalog?\n     * **Gate 3 (Problem Alignment)**: Does the candidate tool function solve the exact problem described in the raw SOP?\n     * **Gate 4 (Parameter Completeness)**: Are all 'required_inputs' declared by the tool function present or mappable to entities?\n\n    * DECISION MANDATE:\n      - IF ALL 4 Gates PASS -> Classify as a Tool Playbook step (`\"step_type\": \"tool_call\"`). Populate `\"platform\"`, `\"provider\"`, `\"function_name\"`, and `\"comm_inputs\"`.\n      - IF ANY Gate FAILS -> Classify as a Command Playbook step (`\"step_type\": \"command\"`). Translate GUI/text steps into exact Windows/Linux CLI shell commands with `\"shell\": \"powershell\"`.\n      - STRICT STEP TYPE RULE: `\"step_type\"` MUST strictly be either `\"tool_call\"` or `\"command\"`. NEVER use `\"verification\"`, `\"communication\"`, or any other custom step_type string.\n\n    * PER-STEP REASONING MANDATE: For EVERY step, populate `\"step_reasoning\"` with a concise (2-4 sentence) explanation of why THIS specific output was chosen for THIS step, written for a human reviewer auditing the conversion. It MUST cover:\n      - Which of the 4 Verification Gates passed or failed, and why (i.e. why `tool_call` vs `command` was chosen here).\n      - If `tool_call`: why this exact `platform`/`function_name` was selected over any other catalog entry that looked plausible, and where each `comm_inputs` value came from (raw SOP text, an inferred entity, or a prior step's `output_variable`).\n      - If `command`: why this exact CLI command (and any `{{{{entities.*}}}}`/`{{{{variables.*}}}}` placeholders) was the correct translation of the SOP instruction.\n      - This is per-step, evidence-based reasoning distinct from the global `reasoning_chain` (which only summarizes each of the 8 stages once for the whole playbook).\n\n2. **Stage 2 (Diagnostic & Remediation Sequence Ordering)**: Ensure non-destructive search/diagnostic steps (e.g. `search_users` or `ipconfig`) come FIRST before any corrective or account modification commands.\n\n3. **Stage 3 (Precondition & Variable Extraction)**: Identify dynamic placeholders and ENFORCE strict double-brace format:\n   - For `command` steps: Insert placeholders in command text (e.g. `{{{{entities.hostname}}}}`, `{{{{entities.executable}}}}`).\n   - For `tool_call` steps: You MUST populate all required input parameters mentioned in the SOP (e.g. `current_password`, `email`, `user_id`) inside `comm_inputs` (e.g. `{{{{ \"password\": \"{{{{entities.current_password}}}}\" }}}}`).\n   - Use `{{{{entities.var_name}}}}` for pre-configured entities (`{{{{entities.user_id}}}}`, `{{{{entities.hostname}}}}`, `{{{{entities.email}}}}`, `{{{{entities.current_password}}}}`) and `{{{{variables.var_name}}}}` for runtime step outputs. DO NOT use un-nested single braces.\n\n4. **Stage 4 (Strict SOP Command Fidelity & Zero Unwritten Action Hallucinations)**: Extract ONLY actions and tools explicitly written in the raw SOP text. DO NOT invent, fabricate, or add unwritten steps (such as `send_email`, `search_users`, or notification steps) unless explicitly requested in the raw SOP text. If not explicitly specified in the raw SOP, set `\"fallback_command\": null`.\n\n5. **Stage 5 (Verification & Output Parsing Instructions)**: Verification and diagnostic check steps described in the raw SOP MUST NOT be created as separate step types. Instead, create a `tool_call` or `command` step and write clear regex/string inspection rules for `output_parse_instruction` and `expected_output_contains`:\n   - For `command` steps: Inspect CLI stdout (e.g. \"If output contains 'SUCCESS', set 'kill_confirmed' to 'true'\").\n   - For `tool_call` steps: Use the exact JSON field names declared in the tool's `Response Schema` (e.g. \"If response contains status='unlocked', set 'unlock_success' to 'true' and store response.user_id in variables.user_id\").\n\n6. **Stage 6 (100% Diagnostic Step Recall Mandate)**: You MUST extract EVERY SINGLE step described in the procedure section of the raw SOP text. Omitting any diagnostic or verification step is STRICTLY PROHIBITED.\n\n7. **Stage 7 (Conditional Branch Mapping & execute_if)**: If the raw SOP specifies conditional skip instructions, map this logic into valid `execute_if` boolean expressions (e.g., `execute_if: variables.account_found == 'true'`).\n\n8. **Stage 8 (Dynamic Timeout Allocation by Action Scope)**: Assign `timeout_seconds` dynamically: Quick Info Read / API Call (30s), System Scan / Diagnostic Audit (90s), Full Repair / Heavy Job (300s).\n\n9. **CRITICAL MANDATE (Omit GUI Window Setup Steps)**: DO NOT generate non-executable GUI setup steps like \"Open Command Prompt\" or \"Open Web Browser\". Start directly with functional actions.\n\n10. **CRITICAL MANDATE (Zero Unwritten Action Addition)**: You are STRICTLY FORBIDDEN from inventing or adding steps/tool calls (such as `send_email` or `search_users`) that are NOT explicitly stated in the raw SOP document.\n\n11. **CRITICAL MANDATE (Omit Manual Human/Verbal Confirmation Steps)**: Human/verbal/ticket confirmation checks, manual admin approvals, and user clarification steps are currently PAUSED. Omit non-automated verbal/ticket confirmation steps and start directly with automated API tool calls (`tool_call`) and CLI system commands (`command`).\n\n\n## OUTPUT JSON SCHEMA MANDATE\nCRITICAL FORMATTING MANDATE:\n- All keys and string values MUST be enclosed in standard DOUBLE QUOTES (\").\n- NEVER use Python single quotes (') for dictionary keys or strings.\n- Return raw, valid JSON only matching this schema:\n```json\n{{\n    \"reasoning_chain\": [\n        \"[Stage 1 Verification Gates]: Gate 1 (Function match) -> Gate 2 (Provider active) -> Gate 3 (Problem aligned) -> Gate 4 (Params complete) -> Result: tool_call | command\",\n        \"[Stage 2 Sequencing]: Ordered search/diagnostic steps before remediation\",\n        \"[Stage 3 Variables]: Formatted {{{{entities.var}}}} placeholders\",\n        \"[Stage 4 Fallbacks]: Mapped fallback actions\",\n        \"[Stage 5 Verification]: Added output parse instructions\",\n        \"[Stage 6 Recall]: Preserved 100% of diagnostic steps\",\n        \"[Stage 7 Branching]: Mapped execute_if conditions\",\n        \"[Stage 8 Timeouts]: Assigned timeout_seconds\"\n    ],\n    \"sop_id\": \"SOP-{{file_name_slug}}-001\",\n    \"title\": \"Title of Playbook\",\n    \"platform\": \"primary platform (e.g. solandra, auth0, airretailer, mock, system)\",\n    \"category\": \"network | storage | system | logins | software | hardware\",\n    \"target_os\": \"windows | linux\",\n    \"description\": \"Comprehensive overview of what this playbook does\",\n    \"required_entities\": [\"user_id\", \"email\", \"hostname\"],\n    \"trigger_words\": [\"unlock\", \"account\", \"login\", \"platform_name\"],\n    \"steps\": [\n        {{\n            \"step_number\": 1,\n            \"sop_step_description\": \"Detailed step objective\",\n            \"step_type\": \"tool_call | command\",\n            \"step_reasoning\": \"Gate 1-4 result and why tool_call/command was chosen; why this exact provider/function/command and comm_inputs/placeholders were used over alternatives.\",\n            \"platform\": \"platform name from REGISTERED API TOOLS CATALOG (only for tool_call, else null)\",\n            \"provider\": \"same as platform (only for tool_call, else null)\",\n            \"function_name\": \"exact_function_name (from REGISTERED API TOOLS CATALOG above, only for tool_call, else null)\",\n            \"comm_inputs\": {{\n                \"user_id\": \"{{{{entities.user_id}}}}\"\n            }},\n            \"command\": \"ipconfig /all (only for command, else null)\",\n            \"shell\": \"powershell (only for command, else null)\",\n            \"fallback_command\": null,\n            \"fallback_sop_id\": null,\n            \"expected_output_contains\": \"unlocked\",\n            \"timeout_seconds\": 30,\n            \"output_variable\": \"step_result\",\n            \"output_parse_instruction\": \"Inspect output/JSON response and set status flags.\",\n            \"execute_if\": null,\n            \"is_dangerous\": false,\n            \"danger_warning\": null\n        }}\n    ]\n}}\n```\n\n## RAW SOP DOCUMENT TEXT TO TRANSLATE:\nReset a Locked-Out User's Password via CoreID Admin Console\nL1 Escalation Runbook — Solandra Corp IT Support\nsop_id\nSOL-PWD-RESET-L1-002\nresolution_type\nescalate_only\naudience\nL1 Support Agent (authorized admin)\nrequired_role\nCoreID: Password Reset Admin\norg\nSolandra Corp\napplication\nCoreID Admin Console (mock)\nPurpose\nWhen a user's self-service password reset fails or the user has no working verification channel, the ticket escalates to L1. An authorized L1 agent with the Password Reset Admin role in CoreID resets the password directly from the admin console.\nBecause the agent's own CoreID session is already MFA-authenticated and role-gated, this flow does not send a one-time verification code to the user. That code-based check exists to authenticate an otherwise-unverified self-service claimant; an admin acting under an authenticated, role-scoped session establishes identity through the caller checklist instead (Step 3).\nProcedure\nInputs\nOpen ticket with the requester's employee ID and manager name on file\nYour own authenticated CoreID session with the Password Reset Admin role assigned\nCaller identity verification checklist (minimum 2 of 4 items matched)\nSteps\nStep 1: Ticket reviewed\nNavigation: Ticketing system → open ticket → Requester Details panel\nConfirm the ticket category reads Password Reset — Escalated, and note the requester's employee ID and manager name. ⚠️ Do not proceed if the ticket is missing a manager name — request it from the requester first.\n\n### Visual Inspection Report (Image 1 (inline, following the text above)) [Confidence: 0.90 / High]:\nThe image shows a screenshot of a computer screen displaying a ticketing system, with a white background and a dark blue sidebar on the left. The ticket is titled \"Ticket #SOL-84213\" and has several fields filled in, including \"Category\", \"Requester\", \"Employee ID\", \"Manager\", \"Department\", and \"Opened\". The \"Category\" field is labeled \"Password Reset - Escalated\".\n\n*   **Ticket Information**\n    *   **Ticket #**: SOL-84213\n    *   **Category**: Password Reset - Escalated\n    *   **Requester**: J. Employee\n    *   **Employee ID**: SOL-04821\n    *   **Manager**: A. Menon\n    *   **Department**: Finance\n    *   **Opened**: Today, 10:42 AM\n*   **Error Messages**\n    *   None visible\n*   **Likely Issue Category and Severity**\n    *   Category: Software/Application\n    *   Severity: Medium\n    *   Reason: The ticket is related to a password reset, which is a common issue in software applications.\n*   **Observed Facts vs. Inference**\n    *   **Observed Facts**\n        *   The ticket is titled \"Ticket #SOL-84213\".\n        *   The category is \"Password Reset - Escalated\".\n        *   The requester is \"J. Employee\".\n        *   The employee ID is \"SOL-04821\".\n        *   The manager is \"A. Menon\".\n        *   The department is \"Finance\".\n        *   The ticket was opened today at 10:42 AM.\n    *   **Inference**\n        *   The issue is likely related to a password reset, as indicated by the category.\n        *   The employee ID and manager information suggest that the issue may be specific to a particular employee or team.\n*   **Confidence Score and Notes**\n    *   **Confidence Score**: 0.95\n    *   **Confidence Level**: High\n    *   **Confidence Reason**: The image is clear and well-lit, with all relevant information visible. The text is legible, and there are no missing or unclear elements.\n\nOverall, the image suggests that the issue is related to a password reset, and the ticket has been escalated to a manager for further action. The employee ID and department information provide additional context, but the exact nature of the issue is not clear from the image alone.\n\nStep 2: User located and lock confirmed\nNavigation: CoreID Admin Console → Users → Search bar\nSearch by employee ID. Confirm the account status badge shows Locked before continuing — do not reset a password on an account that isn't actually locked without further checks.\n\n### Visual Inspection Report (Image 2 (inline, following the text above)) [Confidence: 0.95 / High]:\nThe image shows a screenshot of the CoreID Admin Console, with a navigation menu on the left and a main content area on the right. The main content area is titled \"Users\" and has a search bar at the top.\n\n*   **Navigation Menu:**\n    *   The navigation menu is located on the left side of the image.\n    *   It has several options, including \"Admin Console\", \"Dashboard\", \"Tickets\", \"Users\", \"Reports\", and \"Settings\".\n*   **Main Content Area:**\n    *   The main content area is located on the right side of the image.\n    *   It is titled \"Users\" and has a search bar at the top.\n    *   Below the search bar, there is a table with two columns: \"SOL-04821\" and \"J. Employee\".\n    *   The \"SOL-04821\" column has a value of \"SOL-04821\", while the \"J. Employee\" column has a value of \"SOL-04821 • Finance\".\n    *   There is a red button labeled \"Locked\" in the bottom-right corner of the table.\n*   **Search Bar:**\n    *   The search bar is located at the top of the main content area.\n    *   It has a placeholder text that reads \"SOL-04821\".\n*   **Table:**\n    *   The table has two columns: \"SOL-04821\" and \"J. Employee\".\n    *   The \"SOL-04821\" column has a value of \"SOL-04821\", while the \"J. Employee\" column has a value of \"SOL-04821 • Finance\".\n    *   There is a red button labeled \"Locked\" in the bottom-right corner of the table.\n\n**Extracted Text:**\n\n*   None\n\n**Errors and Warnings:**\n\n*   None visible\n\n**Likely Issue Category and Severity:**\n\n*   Category: Unknown\n*   Severity: Unknown\n\n**Observed Facts vs Inference:**\n\n*   **Observed Facts:**\n    *   The image shows a screenshot of the CoreID Admin Console.\n    *   The navigation menu has several options, including \"Admin Console\", \"Dashboard\", \"Tickets\", \"Users\", \"Reports\", and \"Settings\".\n    *   The main content area is titled \"Users\" and has a search bar at the top.\n    *   The table has two columns: \"SOL-04821\" and \"J. Employee\".\n    *   The \"SOL-04821\" column has a value of \"SOL-04821\", while the \"J. Employee\" column has a value of \"SOL-04821 • Finance\".\n    *   There is a red button labeled \"Locked\" in the bottom-right corner of the table.\n*   **Inference:**\n    *   The user is likely trying to access the \"Users\" page in the CoreID Admin Console.\n    *   The search bar is likely used to filter the list of users.\n    *   The table is likely used to display information about the users.\n    *   The red button labeled \"Locked\" may indicate that the user is locked out or has restricted access.\n\n**Confidence Score and Notes:**\n\n*   CONFIDENCE SCORE: 0.95\n*   CONFIDENCE LEVEL: High\n*   CONFIDENCE REASON: The image is clear and well-lit, and the text is legible. However, there is no additional context or information available to provide a more detailed analysis.\n\nStep 3: Caller identity verified\nNavigation: CoreID Admin Console → user profile → Identity Verification panel\nAsk the caller for at least 2 of: employee ID, manager's name, department, or last 4 digits of the phone on file. ✅ Do not send a one-time code to the user — your own admin session already satisfies the authentication requirement; this checklist is what verifies the caller is who they claim to be. If fewer than 2 items match, stop and escalate to your team lead rather than proceeding.\n\n### Visual Inspection Report (Image 3 (inline, following the text above)) [Confidence: 0.90 / High]:\nThe image shows a screenshot of the CoreID Admin Console, with a list of options for identity verification. The purpose of the image is to provide a visual representation of the different options available for verifying an employee's identity.\n\nHere are the details of the image:\n\n* **CoreID Admin Console**\n\t+ The top-left corner of the image has a dark blue bar with white text that reads \"CoreID\" and \"Admin Console\".\n\t+ Below this, there is a light gray bar with white text that reads \"Signed in as: A. Rao (L1 Support - Password Reset Admin)\".\n* **List of Options**\n\t+ The main content of the image is a list of options for identity verification, titled \"J. Employee - Identity Verification\".\n\t+ The list includes two options:\n\t\t- Employee ID matches ticket\n\t\t- Manager name matches ticket\n\t+ There are also two checkboxes below the list, one for \"Department matches record\" and one for \"Last 4 digits of phone on file\".\n\t+ At the bottom of the image, there is a message that reads \"2 of 4 confirmed - minimum required to proceed\".\n\nOverall, the image provides a clear and concise visual representation of the different options available for verifying an employee's identity in the CoreID Admin Console. The use of checkboxes and a progress bar adds a touch of interactivity to the image, making it more engaging and user-friendly.\n\nStep 4: Temporary password issued\nNavigation: CoreID Admin Console → user profile → Reset Password\nSelect Generate Temporary Password. Leave Force change at next sign-in checked — this is the default and it must not be disabled.\n\n### Visual Inspection Report (Image 4 (inline, following the text above)) [Confidence: 0.90 / High]:\nThe image shows a screenshot of a password reset page for an employee, with a white background and a gray sidebar on the left. The page has a title that reads \"Reset Password - J. Employee\" in black text, and below it, there is a temporary password field with a checkbox next to it that says \"Force change at next sign-in.\" There is also a blue button that says \"Generate Temporary Password.\"\n\nHere are the details of the image:\n\n* **Sidebar:**\n\t+ The sidebar is gray and has a list of options, including \"Admin Console,\" \"Dashboard,\" \"Tickets,\" \"Users,\" \"Reports,\" and \"Settings.\"\n\t+ The \"Users\" option is highlighted in gray.\n* **Password Reset Page:**\n\t+ The page has a white background and a gray border around the edges.\n\t+ The title \"Reset Password - J. Employee\" is in black text at the top of the page.\n\t+ Below the title, there is a temporary password field with a checkbox next to it that says \"Force change at next sign-in.\"\n\t+ There is a blue button that says \"Generate Temporary Password\" below the checkbox.\n* **Error Messages:**\n\t+ There are no error messages visible on the page.\n\nBased on the image, it appears that the user is attempting to reset their password for the J. Employee account. The temporary password field and the checkbox to force a change at the next sign-in suggest that the user is trying to create a new password for the account. The blue button to generate a temporary password is likely used to create a new password for the user.\n\n**Likely Issue Category and Severity:**\n\n* **Category:** Software/Application\n* **Severity:** Medium\n\nThe issue is likely related to the password reset process, and the severity is medium because it may require some technical expertise to resolve.\n\n**Observed Facts vs Inference:**\n\n* **Observed Facts:**\n\t+ The user is on a password reset page.\n\t+ The page has a temporary password field and a checkbox to force a change at the next sign-in.\n\t+ There is a blue button to generate a temporary password.\n* **Inference:**\n\t+ The user is trying to reset their password for the J. Employee account.\n\t+ The temporary password field and the checkbox suggest that the user is trying to create a new password for the account.\n\n**Confidence Score and Notes:**\n\n* **Confidence Score:** 0.95\n* **Confidence Level:** High\n* **Confidence Reason:** The image is clear and well-lit, and the text is legible. There is no missing context or unclear information.\n\nStep 5: Temporary password relayed securely\nNavigation: Phone / voice channel — not logged in CoreID\nRead the temporary password to the caller verbally over the verified phone line. Never send it by email, chat, or ticket comment in plain text. See [CISA's guidance on requiring strong passwords](https://www.cisa.gov/secure-our-world/require-strong-passwords) for the reasoning behind credential-handling policy.\nStep 6: Ticket resolved with audit note\nNavigation: Ticketing system → ticket → Resolution notes\nLog what was done (identity checks matched, temporary password issued, force-change enabled) and set the ticket to Resolved. This note is what satisfies the audit trail requirement — an admin-mediated reset with no note is a compliance gap.\n\n### Visual Inspection Report (Image 5 (inline, following the text above)) [Confidence: 0.90 / High]:\nThe image shows a screenshot of the CoreID Admin Console, with a ticket resolution page open. The page displays the ticket number, status, and resolution notes.\n\n*   **Ticket #SOL-84213 - Resolution**\n    *   The ticket number is SOL-84213.\n    *   The status is Resolved.\n*   **Resolution notes**\n    *   The resolution notes are:\n        *   Identity verified (2 of 4 checks).\n        *   Temporary password issued via CoreID.\n        *   Force-change-at-next-sign-in enabled.\n        *   No OTP sent - admin-mediated reset per policy.\n\nThe image provides a clear view of the ticket resolution page, showing the ticket number, status, and resolution notes. The resolution notes indicate that the identity has been verified, a temporary password has been issued, and force-change-at-next-sign-in has been enabled. Additionally, it is noted that no OTP has been sent, and the admin has mediated the reset per policy.\n\nOutputs\nUser account unlocked with a valid temporary password\nForce-change-at-next-sign-in flag enabled\nTicket resolved with an audit note logged\nFAQs\nQ: What if the caller can't confirm at least 2 of the 4 identity items?\nDo not issue a reset. Escalate to your team lead, or ask the requester to retry self-service once they regain access to a verified phone or email.\nQ: Why doesn't this flow send a verification code the way self-service does?\nThe self-service code exists to authenticate an anonymous claimant with no other proof of identity. Here, identity is established two ways at once: your own CoreID session is already MFA-authenticated and scoped to the Password Reset Admin role, and the caller checklist independently confirms the requester is who they claim to be. Adding a code on top of that verifies nothing new — it would just be an unnecessary step to a channel the caller may not have working anyway, which is usually why the ticket was escalated in the first place.\n\n## LINKED REFERENCE KNOWLEDGE (Scraped from embedded SOP URLs):\n### Reference Web Page [https://www.cisa.gov/secure-our-world/require-strong-passwords]:\nURL: https://www.cisa.gov/secure-our-world/require-strong-passwords\nStatus Code: 200\n\n--- SCRAPED MARKDOWN CONTENT START ---\n[Skip to main content](#main)\n\n![U.S. flag](/profiles/cisad8_gov/themes/custom/gesso/dist/images/us_flag_small.png)\n\nAn official website of the United States government\n\nHere’s how you know\n\nHere’s how you know\n\n![Dot gov](/profiles/cisad8_gov/themes/custom/gesso/dist/images/icon-dot-gov.svg)\n\n**Official websites use .gov**\n\n A **.gov** website belongs to an official government organization in the United States.\n\n![HTTPS](/profiles/cisad8_gov/themes/custom/gesso/dist/images/icon-https.svg)\n\n**Secure .gov websites use HTTPS**\n\n A **lock** (LockA locked padlock) or **https://** means you’ve safely connected to the .gov website. Share sensitive information only on official, secure websites.\n\n[Staying Secure at Events](/staying-secure-large-scale-events)[no-cost Cyber Services](/resources-tools/resources/free-cybersecurity-services-and-tools \"Free Cyber Services\")[secure your business](/secureyourbusiness)[Known Exploited Vulnerabilities Catalog](/kev)[Report A Cyber Issue](/report)\n\n[![Cybersecurity & Infrastructure Security Agency logo America’s Cyber Security Defense Agency National Coordinator For Critical Infrastructure Security and Resilience](/sites/default/files/images/SVG/header_logo_tagline_update.svg)![Cybersecurity & Infrastructure Security Agency logo America’s Cyber Security Defense Agency National Coordinator For Critical Infrastructure Secur"
